[interviewer · ANALYSIS · 5:45] Okay. On what you asked for: COGS is 58% of revenue today, up from 42% two years ago. Labor is 22% of revenue, and it’s been stable. On the data you asked for — I'll come to that shortly. Before we wrap, on what you asked about earlier: Overhead is 14% of revenue, up slightly from 12%. Average revenue per store is $2.4M a year. Check the arithmetic against the 100-to-115 setup: costs going from 76 to roughly 108 is the claim, so how did you get 6.9 for profit, and what is the cost growth?

[candidate · 7:15] Yes — profit of 6.9 is just 6% of the new revenue base of 115, since the 6% is a margin on current revenue, not on the old base. So costs are 115 − 6.9 = 108.1, versus 76 before. That's 32.1 / 76 ≈ 42% cost growth against 15% revenue growth.

And the exhibit confirms where it sits: COGS up 16 points of revenue, labor flat, overhead up 2. So roughly 16 of the 18 points of lost margin are COGS. That's the whole case.

Before I dig into COGS, a scoping question: is that 58% figure a cost ratio only, or do we know whether unit input costs rose? In other words, did Brew & Bean's price per pound of coffee and per gallon of milk actually go up, or is the ratio moving because something else changed?
