[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 10:16] I don't have volume by daypart or product category. Where is the cost-side thinking incomplete, and what could the client do on the revenue side besides raising menu prices?

[candidate · 11:46] Where cost thinking is incomplete: I've focused on COGS and skipped the 2-point overhead rise, which on $480M total revenue is real money — worth knowing if it's corporate headcount or something one-time. I also haven't tested whether input inflation is cyclical or structural, which changes whether you hedge or restructure sourcing. And labor at 22% being "stable" as a ratio actually means labor costs grew 15% with volume — there may be scheduling efficiency there.

Revenue side beyond menu prices: raise average ticket through attachment — food pairings, size upsell, add-ons like syrups and alternative milks, which carry premium pricing. Loyalty programs and subscriptions to lift frequency. Selling whole beans and merchandise at retail margins. Catering and office accounts. And mix shifting toward lower-bean-intensity drinks.

Want me to pull it together into a recommendation?
